ef 17592186425282 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425286 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425318 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1077 "field-2" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425318 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425331 1079 #uuid "cf175a51-5f83-55f9-bec0-896057053ccd" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425276 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425279 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425281 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425289 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425304 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1077 "selection" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425289 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425332 1079 #uuid "ed34ebf7-d0c0-5560-bc6c-feaad1d7a4a3" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425273 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186425278 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1077 "field-2" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1078 #edb/ref 17592186425278 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425333 1079 #uuid "f63bded0-5eca-5ef0-8469-313f1af281d1" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425322 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425323 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1069 "q-333674" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1072 #uuid "6510dfcd-a99e-5730-8a08-a25b49094096" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425334 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =3$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/95/95bb5be106524711e86d2e1ff9cb21d61141c25eb0a2f8958b51c29791806ccc.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425332 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1069 "q-333272" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1072 #uuid "9addc7f5-7ff7-5987-87ac-e05cc5e87102" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425335 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{7t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425329 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1069 "q-333291" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1072 #uuid "c87fc503-31b5-5d30-8770-67dd20112235" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425336 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c521054006aa834b17071f5dd33a97fe977e5d118b771be.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425320 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425324 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425325 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425326 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425327 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425328 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425330 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425331 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1069 "q-333485" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1072 #uuid "f2b9f8e2-b39f-52a6-8433-15b3bb05fe5e" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425337 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t+1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | $0.60$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | $3.58$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425321 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425333 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1069 "q-333667" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has only one eigenvalue. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1072 #uuid "fc362b82-7598-58e4-b9cc-cf1f2a033f38" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true] [17592186425338 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =-2$ and it's negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/94/94f722ae71911d6b6967e55922301e771753078302aef950e1215d1fead75289.png)" 13194139534550 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-07399bf6-90a9-5db9-8898-b1137d5bdf22" 17592186425272 "answer-0dead613-6310-5d03-ae0a-f9d87e3caec5" 17592186425273 "answer-0f7e42fc-0c89-5e3f-acdd-b5f58410b300" 17592186425274 "answer-224da477-1c8f-5938-b87e-1e7e7ebbf8fb" 17592186425275 "answer-22875c88-e98a-556f-bd92-7742a4417f68" 17592186425276 "answer-248c9b5b-4ae7-5277-a8db-7eda9b8f278